AP040 differential testing against QEMU
=======================================

WinUAE cputest v20 corpus replay
--------------------------------

`../run_cputest.py` is the corpus-scale RTL regression harness.  It accepts
the downloaded `data040.zip`, an extraction root, or its `data/` directory.
The archive is intentionally not checked into this repository.

Quick representative gate (64 records from each of integer, trace, FPU, IRQ,
address-error and deliberately odd-vector slices):

    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data040.zip

The native-code Verilator backend is recommended for corpus runs.  It builds
the complete RTL once and then executes the same APR2 slice jobs roughly
25--30 times faster than Icarus on this host:

    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data040.zip --simulator verilator
    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data040.zip --simulator verilator --compile-only

Full corpus, deterministically divided between four workers or machines:

    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data040.zip --simulator verilator \
        --full --shard 0/4 --jobs 2 --resume

Focused reproduction and full codec/oracle audits:

    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data --full --group BasicFPU \
        --instruction 'FABS.*' --slice 0001 --round-limit 2000
    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data040.zip --audit
    ../run_cputest.py /path/to/data040.zip --audit --semantic-audit

The supplied archive is `DATA_VERSION 20`: 587 instruction headers, 1,911
compressed data slices, 1,917,739 cases and 31,522,118 execution rounds.
Both structural and semantic full-corpus audits complete without a decoder
desynchronization.  Newer WinUAE sources use version 24 and are not format
authority for this archive; the decoder follows `main.c` at `f0cf9536~1`.

Each compressed slice has a 16-byte version/start-time/flags/spare envelope.
The old prototype incorrectly treated that envelope as test records; APR2
validates and removes it before decoding.  APR2 jobs are deliberately made
one slice at a time, so a 31.5-million-round run is resumable and never
requires a multi-gigabyte expanded file.  `--shard INDEX/COUNT` assigns whole
slices, preserving all delta and memory history within a slice.

The RTL driver loads the corpus `lmem.dat` and `tmem.dat`, without overwriting
address zero or its operands.  Reset and VBR use synthetic read overlays.  At
the stalled first test fetch it injects D0-D7/A0-A6, USP/ISP/MSP, SR and trace
latches, all FP registers, FPCR/FPSR/FPIAR, and neutral CPU/MMU controls; it
also clears the warm-reset-persistent ATCs.  It drives the Amiga INTREQ-to-IPL
mapping, captures architectural state at `S_EXC0` before exception stacking
changes A7/SR, compares expected memory writes and restores their old values,
and applies the stream's post-round and end-of-test memory actions in order.
Vector 9 contains a real RTE so standalone and handler-stacked trace cases
continue to the primary result.  A nonzero header `exception_vectors` value
is returned for vectors 4 and above, reproducing the ODD_EXC/ODD_IRQ behavior.

The v20 native checker constructs the stacked exception PC from the CPU value
it just captured, rather than from corpus oracle bytes.  APR2 preserves that
contract (frame PC bytes are masked), while normal terminator completion has
an independent exact stacked-PC check.  Cycle counts are not compared: the
040 corpus is functional data and this testbench intentionally uses a simple
one-cycle memory target.  Physical BERR/MMU behavior remains covered by the
directed AP040 exception and MMU suites.

One v20 corpus defect remains observable in memory-source tails.  The
generator resets its access-history indices for each candidate without
undoing setup writes; if every CCR round of that candidate is then discarded,
the memory mutation survives in the generator but no `CT_MEMWRITE` is emitted.
The native v20 runner cannot reproduce that hidden transition either because
it calls `restoreahist()` after each serialized test.  For example,
`BasicFPU/FMOVE.P/0001` test 389 expects an FPEA calculated from `$00080006`,
but the last serialized value is `$80080006` and the native-cleanup value is
`$00080004`.  APR2 follows the serialized generator state (the model which
matches more of the result oracles) and reports such cases instead of masking
the effective-address bytes.  They must not be treated as evidence for an RTL
EA change unless the operand memory is independently reconstructed.

The 2026-08-12 full 1,911-slice Verilator run passes 1,875 slices with no
timeouts or harness errors.  All 1,563 non-BasicFPU/PackedFPU slices pass.
The FPU groups pass 312/348 (BasicFPU 288/320 and PackedFPU 24/28).  The first
mismatch in every one of the 36 failing slices is a memory-EA FPU instruction:
32 opclass-010 loads and four opclass-011 stores.  There is no first failure
in a register-source arithmetic operation.  This classification is
diagnostic, not an automatic waiver: `tb_dat_replay` prints `op=` and the
dispatch-time `ea=`, and `summary.json` records `first_mismatch_opcode`,
`first_mismatch_ea`, `first_mismatch_fpu_opclass`, and
`first_mismatch_memory_ea`.  Failed slices still fail the run so a real
memory-EA RTL regression cannot be hidden by the known corpus limitation.

Two independently reconstructed load cases demonstrate the omitted state.
`BasicFPU/FADD.X/0001` test 182 reads twelve zero bytes from the serialized EA
`$0000BFFF`, although its oracle expects the sticky result and status from a
nonzero tiny addend.  `BasicFPU/FNEG.X/0003` test 203 reads low mantissa
`$2FD2B162` and AP040 preserves it, while the oracle expects `$2FD20000` from
an unrecorded previous rounding/write.  Therefore changing FPU rounding or
underflow behavior to satisfy those records would make self-contained inputs
incorrect.

Every run writes `summary.json`, JUnit `junit.xml`, and one log per slice below
`--work` (default `build/cputest`).  A simulator's zero exit status is not
accepted as success unless its log also contains `ALL TESTS PASSED`.  Verilator
artifacts and reports live below `build/cputest/verilator`, independent of the
Icarus files.  Each passing resume log has a sidecar identity covering the RTL
binary, simulator, decoder, corpus slice, memory images and record limit, so a
smoke result cannot accidentally satisfy a full-corpus `--resume` run.

run_diff.sh generates random integer programs (gen_diff.py), runs the
same flat binary on the AP040 (verilated tb_ap040_program) and on
qemu-system-m68k -M virt -cpu m68040, and compares the $3000-$3FFF
memory window: sandbox data, the final register file image and the
final CCR.

The per-instruction CCR trace ($3800-$3EFF) is generated but NOT
compared by default. QEMU 11.0.2's m68k lazy flag evaluation was found
to produce flags that contradict the M68000PRM (and QEMU's own behavior
in isolated sequences) in at least these shapes, all reproduced in this
directory during bring-up:

  - SUBX with X=0, Z(old)=0 and result 0 reports Z=1 when the previous
    flag producer was a lazily evaluated ALU op ("work/qbug.s" repro:
    PRM says Z unchanged, i.e. 0)
  - NEGX C reported 0 for a nonzero result when preceded by a bitfield
    instruction with a register offset (stream context only)
  - MULU.L overflow V lost in similar stream contexts, although the
    isolated case ("work/mulv.s") is handled correctly

The AP040 side of each arbitration was confirmed against hand-computed
PRM semantics ("work/truth.s"). AP040 flag correctness is regression
tested by tests/ap040/asm/* with exact CCR expectations; the
differential run verifies data paths, EA computation and instruction
sequencing, where QEMU is reliable.

Usage:
  verilator --binary --timing ... tb_ap040_program.v rtl/ap040/*.v
  SIM=/path/to/Vtb_ap040_program ./run_diff.sh 1 50 150

Floating-point differential (gen_fpdiff.py / run_fpdiff.sh)
----------------------------------------------------------
Random FP programs: a pool of normalized extended/single/double constants,
FP0-FP7 loaded from it, then a stream of FADD/FSUB/FMUL/FDIV/FSQRT/FABS/
FNEG/FMOVE (all formats, register and memory) with FPCR rounding changes,
ending in FMOVEM.X of all eight registers plus FPCR and the FPSR condition
codes into the compared window.  Operand ranges are constrained so no
operation overflows, underflows, denormalizes, or produces Inf/NaN: those
are exactly the cases where AP040 (which traps unsupported data types to
vector 55 and flushes underflow) and qemu (which computes them) differ by
design rather than by defect.

  ./run_fpdiff.sh <first_seed> <last_seed> [ops]     # SIM=<verilated tb>

FPDIFF_MODE selects coverage:
  unset   extended precision, all rounding modes, all instructions
  arith   all precisions and modes, arithmetic only (no register FMOVE)
  all     everything -- diverges, see below

qemu 11 deviation found 2026-08-04: **qemu does not apply the FPCR rounding
precision (bits 7:6) to FMOVE into a floating-point register.**  A plain
FMOVE is an arithmetic instruction for rounding purposes and must round its
result like any other; WinUAE's reference `fp_move(a, b, prec)` sets the
rounding precision around the move, and AP040 does the same.  Minimal
repro: with FPCR=$40 (single, round to nearest), FMOVE.X of the extended
value $3FFF/$8000000000000FFF gives $8000000000000000 on AP040 and on real
040 silicon, but $8000000000000FFF on qemu.  Because the wrong operand then
feeds every later instruction, a single such FMOVE makes an entire random
program diverge; that is why reduced precision is only generated in the
modes above.  AP040's behaviour is regression-covered by t_fpu.s tests
88-92 instead.

With that one case excluded, AP040 and qemu agree exactly: arithmetic
across all four rounding modes and all three precisions (FPDIFF_MODE=arith)
and the default mode both match on every seed tried.

MMU differential (gen_mmudiff.py / run_mmudiff.sh)
--------------------------------------------------
Random three-level translation setups inside the 64K the testbench models:
pages 0-5 identity (code, evidence window, stack/tables, result arrays),
pages 6-11 randomly identity / remapped into pages 12-14 / write protected,
page 15 left alone because it holds the testbench protocol registers.  The
program enables translation, runs random translated reads, writes and PTEST
probes, disables translation and copies the evidence into the compared
window: read-back values, the page table itself (whose U/M history bits the
hardware updates during the walks), the root and pointer descriptors, and
the MMUSR results.

  ./run_mmudiff.sh <first_seed> <last_seed> [ops]    # SIM=<verilated tb>

Writes are never generated against a write-protected page and all mappings
are resident, so no access error is taken: fault frames are format $7 on
AP040 and a different shape on qemu, and t_mmu.s covers them instead.

qemu 11 deviation found 2026-08-04, worked around in the generator:

  1. qemu never sets the M (modified) history bit in a page descriptor.
     AP040 sets it on the first write to a page, as the 68040 must, which
     also shows up in MMUSR; t_mmu.s tests 5-7 cover it.  The generator
     clears the M bit in the evidence copies so the rest still compares.

The generator issues PFLUSHA after every TC write because the MC68040 does
not invalidate either ATC when an MMU register is read or written.  Software
must flush explicitly when the new register value can change translations;
t_mmu.s tests 36, 37, and 60 pin that behavior.

With those behaviors accounted for, AP040 and qemu agree exactly on translation,
write protection, the U history bits on all three table levels, and PTEST
MMUSR results across every seed tried (20 seeds x 24 operations).

Further qemu 11 FP deviations found 2026-08-04 while closing the FPU gaps:

  3. Reduced rounding precision narrows the significand, NOT the exponent
     range.  softfloat's SOFTFLOAT_68K path checks overflow/underflow
     against 0x7FFE for every rounding precision, and its roundMask only
     trims mantissa bits.  AP040 originally clamped overflow/underflow at
     the single/double exponent boundaries; the FP differential caught it
     and it is fixed (t_fpu.s covers the rounding, and FPDIFF_MODE=unfl
     exercises tiny operands at reduced precision).
  4. Extended subnormals: an exponent field of zero scales by 2^-16382,
     i.e. softfloat normalizes a subnormal to exponent (1 - shiftCount)
     and packs an underflowed result by shifting right (1 - exponent).
     qemu encodes such results one power of two too large, and does not
     set UNFL.  Minimal repro: 2^-16380 * 2^-13 = 2^-16393 gives exponent
     0 with significand $0010000000000000 on AP040 (and per softfloat),
     $0020000000000000 on qemu.  t_fpu.s tests 93-99 pin AP040's encoding.

softfloat_oracle.cpp
--------------------
Drives WinUAE's own softfloat (SOFTFLOAT_68K) directly, so an AP040 result
can be checked against the 68040 reference code rather than against qemu,
whose FPSR is weak and whose NaN-to-integer result is wrong.  Build:

  cd /path/to/WinUAE
  g++ -O0 -w -I. -o sfcheck /path/to/softfloat_oracle.cpp softfloat/softfloat.cpp

Used 2026-08-20 to confirm FMOVE.L FPn,Dn: values AND the OPERR/INEX2
flags match AP040 on all 13 probes across the 2^31 boundary, including
the rule that a NaN converts to its HIGH MANTISSA LONGWORD.
